      <article id="mid-late-2026" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Mid to late 2026</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Retro archives and CGA graphics</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            Recently there have been some significant code rewrites for performance and compatibility improvements while handling uploads of obscure media image, text, and compression formats for the Commodore Amiga and MS-DOS systems.
            There should now be fewer warnings when submitting images stored in IFF, ILBM, and PBM containers (EA Deluxe Paint) and ZIP, LHA, and LZH archives.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also introduced is native support for the oddball PKZIP methods that existed in the BBS era before "DEFLATE" became the default.
            Previously, the website relied on <a href="https://www.hanshq.net/zip.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hwzip</a>, which while functional, didn't support the preservation of archive sub-folders or file last modification dates.
            This has been fixed with a native Go implementation of "EXPAND", "EXPLODE", and "UNSHRINK"!
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, there is a new, better way of detecting binary texts, hopefully leading to fewer instances of the web application displaying raw and binary text files incorrectly.
            Binary texts are screen data dumps of the video memory for CGA, EGA, and VGA text modes, which use a byte-pair for each character on screen.
            The first byte is usually a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_page_437" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CP-437 character code point</a> that uses 256 possible codes.
            The second byte is an attribute value for foreground, background colors, text blinking, and color intensity, that also uses all 256 possible bit combinations.
            Each screen dump is usually 80 columns by 25 rows. 80×25 equals 2000 characters, combined with 2000 attribute bytes being saved as a 4000 byte file, or 4096 bytes (4KB) with 96 appended placeholder bytes.
            While ANSI art and binary text outputs look similar, the simplicity of binary text meant it was far easier to parse and more performant on PCs of the 1980s.
            However, this was at the expense of the fixed file size, as even displaying a single character onscreen would require the 4000 byte file size.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="mid-2026" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Mid 2026</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">More write-ups</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            Recently the site had some improvements to the <em>Textfile content</em> containers that get used for displaying NFOs, <code>file_id.diz</code>, text magazine issues, etc. Now the webapp will display up to two separate text files, as well as the <code>file_id.diz</code> in any archive.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also updated were the group biographies, known as tidbits, that are used for release groups and magazines. Some major write-ups were done for <a href="/g/courier-weektop-scorecard">Courier Weektop Scorecard</a> and its partner in crime, the <a href="/g/the-net-monkey-weekly-report">NetMonkey Weekly Review</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="early-mid-2026" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Early to mid 2026</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">PCBoard BBS systems</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            Added comprehensive support for PCBoard files. While these have always existed on the site, they can now be filtered and viewed on their own pages —
            either as a <a href="/files/pcboard">PCBoard&nbsp;collection</a>, as <a href="/files/pcboard-ppe">PPE&nbsp;tools</a> and applications,
            or as <a href="/files/pcboard-text">color&nbsp;text&nbsp;files</a> using <code>@X</code> codes.
            Finally, these <code>@X</code> codes are now rendered as colored text in the <em>readme or information text</em> sections.
          </p>
          <p>
            The user interface of the <a href="/">Intertwined milestones of the PC industry and the Scene</a> has been improved.
            Clicking either the highlight buttons or the milestone dates will pop up a modal with the content for easier readability.
          </p>
          <p>
            And finally, a long-requested feature: <a href="/api">we now offer</a> a RESTful API in JSON.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="early-2026" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Early 2026</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Milestones refresh</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The new year of 2026 sees a large update to the <a href="/">Intertwined milestones of the PC industry and the Scene</a>.
            There are both new articles and several rewrites for entries from the late 1970s and 1980s:
            The first copy protections,
            Widespread disk copying leads to copy protections,
            The first groups,
            The earliest cracktros,
            Commercial Disk Copy Protections,
            The year of the Commodore 64,
            The Berlin Bear controversy, and more!
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="late-2025" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Late 2025</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">ANSI text art</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The last half of the year has seen the introduction of a much-desired ANSI to HTML conversion sitewide.
            All the glorious BBS ads from the 1980s and 1990s are now properly displayed as text!
          </p>
          <p>
            Following this, there has been a major upload of the many ANSI and binary text artworks created by the prolific artgroups of the 1990s,
            <a href="/g/insane-creators-enterprise">iCE Advertisements</a> and <a href="/g/acid-productions">ACiD Productions</a>.
            They're now the <a href="/releaser">top two groups</a> for total number of artifacts on the site.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also, the plain text reader has been updated to use a number of legacy fonts depending on the artifact.
            Fonts include the IBM MDA for PC texts from the 1980s, and an IBM CGA font for ANSI <a href="/f/ba2bcbb">art</a> from the same period.
            Amiga texts now have a more appropriate <a href="/f/ab1daf8">dark grey background</a>,
            while <a href="/f/b82d002">Amiga ANSI</a> can be viewed using the MicroKnight-styled font.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="mid-2025" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Mid 2025</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Top-tier 90s groups</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The mid-year sees another discovery of an extensive Scene releases backup, captured from the late '90s.
            That has helped us <a href="/files/new-uploads">improve</a> the collection on our site.
          </p>
          <p>
            So far, using these compact disc backups, we have been able to flesh out the major game groups of 1996,
            including the replacement of incomplete or broken text files — a common problem for releases that were butchered by third parties and shared online.
            Until now, without these nearly 30-year-old original release archives, we were unable to verify or fix these problematic artifacts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Targeted top-tier game groups include
            <a href="/g/hybrid">Hybrid</a>, <a href="/g/prestige">Prestige</a>,
            <a href="/g/paradigm">Paradigm</a>, <a href="/g/class">Class</a>,
            <a href="/g/razor-1911">Razor 1911</a>, and a collection of FTP site ads. You can follow along using the <a href="/files/new-uploads">new uploads link</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also, a new <a href="/brokentexts">Broken text files</a> FAQ page has been added to the site.
            In it, we explain the corrupt and incorrect texts and NFO files on the site — why they exist and how they came about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="early-2025" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Early 2025</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">HMS Bounty continues</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The new year has continued to see new additions to the website.
            The continued inclusion of scene files backed up on the HMS Bounty BBS tapes has been a significant focus,
            and we are now up to the year 1994, with these releases likely continuing on for a few more years.
          </p>
          <p>
            These new discoveries have also meant some of the milestones listed on the <a href="/">home welcome</a> page have been revised,
            and the page now offers direct links to the highlights, such as <em><a href="/#the-first-groups">The first groups</a></em>.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a new <a href="/areacodes">BBS area codes page</a> for North America with a complete list of districts, as well as search and lookup features.
          </p>
          <p>
            On the backend, the site has expanded its support for more archive and compression types for hosted artifacts, including:
            <code>.cab</code>, <code>.zst</code>, <code>.xz</code>, <code>.arj</code>, <code>.arc</code>, <code>.7z</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="late-2024" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Late 2024</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Thousands of new artifacts</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            In the second half of 2024, we added thousands of new artifacts of historical importance to the scene to the website.
            These include many new <a href="/files/how-to">Unprotect textfiles</a>, a couple intros [<a href="/f/ab22918">1</a>,
            <a href="/f/ad27738">2</a>] from the early to late 1980s,
            and <a href="/files/oldest">Scene releases</a> such as NFOs, trainers, and intros from 1988 to the early 1990s.
          </p>
          <p>
            The unprotected texts were anonymously submitted — thank you!
          </p>
          <p>
            The 1988+ files came from the dump of "<a href="/g/hms-bounty-bbs">The HMS Bounty</a>" BBS backup tapes,
            which an individual* discovered in storage and painstakingly digitized and assembled for online preservation.
            <br><span class="small text-body-secondary">* A massive thank you to you!</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

